The issue is this statement, which appears in all three report-writing steps:
FILE CCDTXT2 HEADER=NEWPAGE;
HEADER= requires a file with the PRINT attribute, which your statement is missing. Your RETURN; statements before and after NEWPAGE: are already present. SAS FILE statement documentation
Update the statements as follows:
LNCCD002 — first report, replace the output file:
FILE CCDTXT2 PRINT HEADER=NEWPAGE
     LINESIZE=133 PAGESIZE=60;
Inside %PROC15 — append the optional report:
FILE CCDTXT2 PRINT MOD HEADER=NEWPAGE
     LINESIZE=133 PAGESIZE=60;
LNCCD004 — append the NPL report:
FILE CCDTXT2 PRINT MOD HEADER=NEWPAGE
     LINESIZE=133 PAGESIZE=60;
PRINT addresses the missing attribute. The explicit page dimensions accommodate your report layout. MOD preserves earlier reports when subsequent DATA steps write to the same file; without it, the default is to replace the file. SAS documentation
Keep your existing header structure:
  RETURN;

  NEWPAGE:
    PAGECNT+1;
    /* Existing header PUT statements */
  RETURN;
RUN;
You should not need to increase /STACK= for this issue. With your current date, 31AUG26, %PROC15 does not generate a report, but its FILE statement should still be corrected for runs on the 15th.
